from (values
('127.751.d.12.E',3,'A review board studies whether an officer who fired a weapon faced an imminent threat of death or serious injury. This is:','["traffic review", "payroll review", "evaluating deadly force", "budget review"]'::jsonb,2,'Deadly force requires grave threat.','original'),
('127.751.d.12.E',4,'Deadly force is generally justified only to stop:','["a verbal argument", "a minor theft", "a parking violation", "an imminent threat of death or serious bodily injury"]'::jsonb,3,'Deadly force is a last resort.','original'),
('127.751.d.13.A',3,'A student practices a pat-down of a role-player immediately after an arrest to find weapons and evidence. This is:','["a search incident to arrest", "a checkpoint", "a random frisk", "a home search"]'::jsonb,0,'Search incident to arrest is lawful.','original'),
('127.751.d.13.A',4,'The two main purposes of a search incident to arrest are officer safety and:','["raising cash", "preserving evidence", "embarrassment", "taking property"]'::jsonb,1,'Safety and evidence justify it.','original'),
('127.751.d.13.B',3,'An officer seat-belts a prisoner, checks the back seat before and after the trip and keeps the prisoner in view. This is:','["ignoring rights", "neglect", "safe transport without violating personal rights", "careless driving"]'::jsonb,2,'Safe, lawful transport.','original'),
('127.751.d.13.B',4,'Before placing a prisoner in a patrol car, an officer should:','["remove the seat belts", "open the windows", "skip the search", "search the back seat for items left behind"]'::jsonb,3,'Prisoners can hide items.','original'),
('127.751.d.13.C',3,'A cadet compares handcuffs, flex cuffs and leg restraints for a transport. This is:','["methods of restraining a person during transport", "traffic control", "radio use", "hiding evidence"]'::jsonb,0,'Restraints must be proper and safe.','original'),
('127.751.d.13.C',4,'When using handcuffs, an officer should check that they:','["are loose", "are secure but not too tight and are double-locked", "are removed", "are rusty"]'::jsonb,1,'Check fit and lock.','original'),
('127.751.d.13.D',3,'An officer drives a lost 9-year-old home and a juvenile in custody to a detention center. This is:','["a parade", "a pursuit", "transporting non-custodial persons and juveniles", "a stakeout"]'::jsonb,2,'Different people require different procedures.','original'),
('127.751.d.13.D',4,'Compared with transporting an adult prisoner, transporting a juvenile usually requires:','["skipping notice", "mixing with adults", "no policy", "following juvenile procedures and separating from adults"]'::jsonb,3,'Juvenile rules apply.','original'),
('127.751.d.14.A',3,'Two students practice a calm, structured interview with a witness and a more formal interrogation of a suspect. This demonstrates:','["interviewing and interrogation techniques", "traffic control", "parking", "filing"]'::jsonb,0,'Techniques differ by role.','original'),
('127.751.d.14.A',4,'An interview differs from an interrogation in that an interview is generally:','["illegal", "information-gathering and non-accusatory", "forceful", "secret"]'::jsonb,1,'Interviews gather information.','original'),
('127.751.d.14.B',3,'A trainee practices gentle, age-appropriate questions with a child who witnessed a crime. This is:','["skipping the child", "a trial", "simulating interviews of rape victims and child witnesses", "aggressive questioning"]'::jsonb,2,'Sensitive interviews need care.','original'),
('127.751.d.14.B',4,'When interviewing a child victim, officers should:','["suggest answers", "pressure the child", "use legal terms", "use simple language and avoid leading questions"]'::jsonb,3,'Avoid leading and confusing language.','original'),
('127.751.d.15.A',3,'A student photographs tire marks, notes road conditions and measures vehicle positions at a mock crash. This is:','["recording crash scene evidence with standard procedures", "skipping evidence", "cleaning the road", "ignoring marks"]'::jsonb,0,'Standard reports preserve evidence.','original'),
('127.751.d.15.A',4,'At a crash scene, an officer should record:','["guesses", "measurements, positions and photographs", "opinions only", "gossip"]'::jsonb,1,'Facts are recorded.','original'),
('127.751.d.15.B',3,'A class applies traffic laws to skid marks and impact points to find who was at fault. This is:','["guessing", "spreading rumors", "analyzing crash scene evidence using laws and procedures", "ignoring evidence"]'::jsonb,2,'Analysis ties evidence to law.','original'),
('127.751.d.15.B',4,'Analyzing a crash scene involves comparing evidence to:','["fashion trends", "radio ads", "rumors", "traffic law and standard investigation procedures"]'::jsonb,3,'Evidence is evaluated under law.','original'),
('127.751.d.15.C',3,'A student uses a skid distance and friction value to estimate a vehicle''s speed. This is:','["calculating speed, velocity, time and distance", "dancing", "story writing", "drawing"]'::jsonb,0,'Math estimates speed.','original'),
('127.751.d.15.C',4,'If a car travels 60 feet in 2 seconds, its average speed is:','["58 feet per second", "30 feet per second", "62 feet per second", "120 feet per second"]'::jsonb,1,'Speed = distance/time.','original'),
('127.751.d.15.D',3,'Using a template, a student draws a top-view scale diagram of two cars at impact. This is:','["tracing a map", "sketching a pet", "drawing scale diagrams of simulated collisions", "painting"]'::jsonb,2,'Scale diagrams show positions.','original'),
('127.751.d.15.D',4,'If 1 inch on a diagram represents 10 feet, a car 20 feet from a curb is drawn:','["10 inches", "20 inches", "0.5 inches", "2 inches from the curb"]'::jsonb,3,'20 ÷ 10 = 2.','original'),
('127.751.d.15.E',3,'A trainee studies gouge marks, debris fields and final rest positions to explain how a collision happened. This is:','["interpreting crash scene evidence", "ignoring evidence", "burying clues", "guessing"]'::jsonb,0,'Evidence tells the story.','original'),
('127.751.d.15.E',4,'A debris field after a crash helps investigators find:','["the weather forecast", "the area of impact", "the driver''s job", "the car''s age"]'::jsonb,1,'Debris marks impact area.','original'),
('127.751.d.16.A',3,'At a multi-agency exercise, one commander directs police, fire and EMS under a common structure. This shows knowledge of the:','["payroll system", "grading system", "incident command system", "traffic system"]'::jsonb,2,'ICS organizes response.','original'),
('127.751.d.16.A',4,'The incident commander is responsible for:','["only traffic", "only media", "only cleanup", "overall management of the incident"]'::jsonb,3,'IC has overall responsibility.','original'),
('127.751.d.16.B',3,'During a tornado response, police coordinate with local, state and federal agencies and private utility crews. This is:','["coordinating with response partners at all levels of government and the private sector", "avoiding help", "competing", "working alone"]'::jsonb,0,'Partnership is needed.','original'),
('127.751.d.16.B',4,'Coordination among agencies matters in a large disaster because it:','["slows response", "avoids duplication and gaps in response", "blocks help", "adds confusion"]'::jsonb,1,'Coordination improves efficiency.','original'),
('127.751.d.16.C',3,'Students review ICS features such as unity of command and manageable span of control in a tabletop. This is:','["a parade", "an exam", "evaluating ICS applications and organizational principles", "a snack break"]'::jsonb,2,'Principles shape structure.','original'),
('127.751.d.16.C',4,'In ICS, ''span of control'' refers to:','["the length of a hose", "the range of a radio", "the radius of a fire", "the number of people one supervisor can effectively manage"]'::jsonb,3,'A supervisor oversees about 3 to 7.','original'),
('127.751.d.16.D',3,'A class uses FEMA''s incident command structure to set up planning, operations, logistics and finance sections for a mock flood. This is:','["applying the FEMA incident command structure", "skipping roles", "ignoring structure", "random planning"]'::jsonb,0,'ICS sections handle functions.','original'),
('127.751.d.16.D',4,'The ICS section that tracks costs and compensation is:','["Operations", "Finance/Administration", "Safety", "Planning"]'::jsonb,1,'Finance handles costs.','original'),
('127.751.d.17.A',3,'A student reads placards and identifies a flammable liquid versus a corrosive. This is:','["mapping", "parking", "identifying and classifying hazardous materials", "photography"]'::jsonb,2,'HazMat has classes.','original'),
('127.751.d.17.A',4,'A diamond-shaped placard on a truck is used to:','["show weight", "advertise", "show origin", "show the type of hazard being carried"]'::jsonb,3,'Placards communicate hazard classes.','original'),
('127.751.d.17.B',3,'In a drill with a suspicious device, officers evacuate, keep radios off near it and call the bomb squad. This follows:','["protocols for responding to explosive materials", "touching the device", "panicking", "ignoring"]'::jsonb,0,'Do not touch; evacuate.','original'),
('127.751.d.17.B',4,'Officers who find a suspected explosive device should:','["open it", "isolate the area and call specialized responders", "shake it", "move it"]'::jsonb,1,'Specialists handle explosives.','original'),
('127.751.d.17.C',3,'Dispatch receives a call that a bomb has been placed in a school. This requires:','["hanging up", "ignoring it", "procedures for responding to bomb threats and suspicious objects", "giggling"]'::jsonb,2,'Threats are taken seriously.','original'),
('127.751.d.17.C',4,'When a suspicious package is found, a good first step is to:','["tear it open", "carry it outside", "kick it", "evacuate to a safe distance and notify authorities"]'::jsonb,3,'Distance and notice protect people.','original'),
('127.751.d.17.D',3,'Teams sweep a mock building room by room for hidden devices, marking what''s searched. This is:','["a building and property search for explosives", "a training exam", "an auction", "a lunch plan"]'::jsonb,0,'Systematic sweeps find devices.','original'),
('127.751.d.17.D',4,'During a search for an explosive device, officers should avoid using:','["flashlights", "radios or cell phones close to a suspected device", "notebooks", "clipboards"]'::jsonb,1,'Radio waves can trigger devices.','original'),
('127.751.d.17.E',3,'An officer responds to an overturned tanker leaking an unknown liquid and sets up a perimeter upwind. This is:','["a parade", "a shift change", "responding to a hazardous material incident", "a stakeout"]'::jsonb,2,'HazMat response begins with isolation.','original'),
('127.751.d.17.E',4,'At a hazmat incident, officers should approach from:','["inside the cloud", "downhill", "downwind", "upwind and uphill"]'::jsonb,3,'Upwind and uphill limit exposure.','original'),
('127.751.d.18.A',3,'A student studies how to protect power plants, water systems and bridges from attack. This is:','["critical infrastructure protection techniques", "gardening", "farming", "fashion"]'::jsonb,0,'Infrastructure is vital.','original'),
('127.751.d.18.A',4,'Critical infrastructure includes systems such as:','["snack bars", "power grids and water supplies", "toys", "board games"]'::jsonb,1,'They support daily life.','original'),
('127.751.d.18.B',3,'A team builds a plan with fences, cameras, patrols and access control for a rail yard. This is:','["a lunch plan", "a trip plan", "a plan for protecting a potential target", "a party plan"]'::jsonb,2,'Layered security protects targets.','original'),
('127.751.d.18.B',4,'Layered security for a target includes:','["decorations", "banners", "souvenirs", "barriers, surveillance and controlled access"]'::jsonb,3,'Layers deter attacks.','original'),
('127.751.d.19.A',3,'A class explores bomb-disposal robots and body cameras. This is researching:','["new technologies used in law enforcement", "old gear", "fashion", "music"]'::jsonb,0,'Technology changes tactics.','original'),
('127.751.d.19.A',4,'A bomb-disposal robot helps officers by:','["filing records", "handling dangerous devices from a safe distance", "writing reports", "driving patrol"]'::jsonb,1,'Robots keep people safe.','original'),
('127.751.d.19.B',3,'An experienced officer takes legal updates, tactics refreshers and leadership courses every year. This shows the importance of:','["skipping classes", "quitting", "continuing education in law enforcement", "avoiding change"]'::jsonb,2,'Law and tactics change.','original'),
('127.751.d.19.B',4,'Continuing education helps officers because laws and best practices:','["are always the same", "are unimportant", "never change", "change over time"]'::jsonb,3,'Training keeps skills current.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.A',3,'A student explains why an officer needs probable cause to stop and arrest a suspect, and reasonable suspicion for a brief stop. This applies:','["legal justification and probable cause for field activities", "a guess", "a rumor", "a hunch"]'::jsonb,0,'Different standards apply.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.A',4,'Reasonable suspicion is required for:','["an arrest", "a brief investigative stop", "a conviction", "a sentence"]'::jsonb,1,'Terry stop standard.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.B',3,'Two teams practice a misdemeanor stop for a taillight violation and a high-risk stop of a stolen car. This is:','["parking", "patrol briefings", "simulating misdemeanor and high-risk traffic stops", "walking beats"]'::jsonb,2,'Stops differ by risk.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.B',4,'During a high-risk stop, officers commonly:','["skip commands", "approach casually", "use no cover", "use cover, give commands and approach with caution"]'::jsonb,3,'High-risk stops require tactics.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.C',3,'A department''s policy limits when officers may chase a fleeing car, motorcycle or on foot. These are:','["pursuit procedures", "sick leave", "lunch rules", "dress code"]'::jsonb,0,'Pursuits are risky.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
