('127.751.d.20.C',4,'Before continuing a vehicle pursuit, an officer weighs:','["lunch time", "the danger of the chase against the seriousness of the offense", "car color", "radio volume"]'::jsonb,1,'Risk-benefit decides.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.D',3,'A dispatcher says a burglary just happened and then reports a burglary in progress. Students simulate responding to:','["a parade", "a shift change", "a delayed crime and a crime in progress", "a noise only"]'::jsonb,2,'Response differs by timing.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.D',4,'Compared with a delayed crime, a crime in progress usually requires:','["no response", "only a report by mail", "a slower response", "a faster response to catch the suspect"]'::jsonb,3,'In progress = urgent.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.E',3,'In training, two officers methodically clear a vacant warehouse after an alarm. This is:','["conducting a building search", "a drive", "a vacation", "a parade"]'::jsonb,0,'Building searches use teamwork.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.E',4,'A key rule when searching a building is to:','["split up with no contact", "communicate and cover each other while moving", "turn off radios", "run ahead"]'::jsonb,1,'Communication prevents blue-on-blue.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.F',3,'An officer arrests a man under a judge''s signed warrant, while another arrests a man who just committed a felony in view. This simulates:','["patrol scheduling", "traffic stops", "warrant and warrantless arrests", "civil service"]'::jsonb,2,'Both types are lawful under conditions.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.F',4,'A warrantless arrest is generally allowed when an officer:','["is bored", "received a rumor", "has a hunch", "has probable cause that a crime was committed in their presence"]'::jsonb,3,'Offenses in view allow arrest.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.G',3,'A class compares how to handle a call with a single officer, a pair, and a team. This differentiates:','["procedures for one-person, two-person and multi-officer units", "patrol cars only", "clothing", "vehicles"]'::jsonb,0,'Staffing changes tactics.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.G',4,'A one-person unit responding to a dangerous call should usually:','["ignore the call", "wait for backup before making contact", "avoid radio", "rush in alone"]'::jsonb,1,'Backup improves safety.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.H',3,'Students compare how they respond to something they see themselves, a dispatched call, and a call from a citizen at the station. This compares:','["weather", "shift length", "patrol responses to on-view and dispatched offenses", "parking"]'::jsonb,2,'Response varies by source.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.H',4,'An on-view offense is one that the officer:','["hears about later", "reads in the news", "is told by letter", "sees personally"]'::jsonb,3,'On-view means witnessed.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.I',3,'A cadet in a patrol-car role-play buckles up, checks surroundings and keeps hands visible. This shows being:','["safety conscious as a patrol officer", "reckless", "careless", "distracted"]'::jsonb,0,'Safety habits save lives.','original'),
('127.751.d.20.I',4,'Wearing a seat belt and a vest are examples of:','["extra duties", "officer safety habits", "fashion", "rituals"]'::jsonb,1,'Basic safety practices.','original'),
('127.751.d.21.A',3,'A class traces how neighborhood foot patrols and citizen partnerships grew from the 1960s to today. This explores the:','["highway design", "tax reform", "development of community policing", "prison building"]'::jsonb,2,'Community policing evolved over time.','original'),
('127.751.d.21.A',4,'Community policing grew from the idea that police and citizens should:','["avoid each other", "compete", "hide", "work together to solve problems"]'::jsonb,3,'Partnership is the core.','original'),
('127.751.d.21.B',3,'A student studies how an officer stationed at a high school counsels students and teaches safety lessons. This evaluates the:','["role of school resource officers", "game wardens", "jailers", "traffic officers"]'::jsonb,0,'SROs work in schools.','original'),
('127.751.d.21.B',4,'A school resource officer serves as a law enforcement officer, a:','["bus driver", "teacher and counselor", "cook", "janitor"]'::jsonb,1,'The SRO triad includes teaching and counseling.','original'),
('127.751.d.21.C',3,'A neighborhood service officer helps residents with graffiti, code issues and block meetings. This evaluates the:','["prison guards", "jail transport", "role of neighborhood service officers", "traffic court"]'::jsonb,2,'NSOs build local ties.','original'),
('127.751.d.21.C',4,'Neighborhood service officers focus on:','["evidence storage", "highway chases", "court security", "community problems and relationships in one area"]'::jsonb,3,'They address local concerns.','original'),
('127.751.d.21.D',3,'A crime prevention officer visits classrooms with a safety-dog mascot to teach children about stranger danger. This evaluates:','["the role of crime prevention officers", "jail officers", "traffic officers", "SWAT"]'::jsonb,0,'Prevention officers educate.','original'),
('127.751.d.21.D',4,'The purpose of crime prevention programs like Neighborhood Watch is to:','["spread fear", "reduce crime by involving residents", "ignore crime", "replace police"]'::jsonb,1,'Involved citizens help prevent crime.','original'),
('127.751.d.21.E',3,'A department''s spokesperson issues a statement and answers reporter questions after a major incident. This evaluates the:','["dispatcher", "patrol officer", "responsibilities of the public information officer", "evidence tech"]'::jsonb,2,'PIOs communicate with the media.','original'),
('127.751.d.21.E',4,'A public information officer must make sure that released information is:','["gossip", "personal opinion", "speculative", "accurate and does not harm the investigation"]'::jsonb,3,'Accuracy and care.','original'),
('127.751.d.21.F',3,'A team maps burglaries by street and time, then recommends better lighting and neighborhood watch. This is a:','["crime prevention analysis", "menu review", "garden plan", "tax audit"]'::jsonb,0,'Analysis finds patterns.','original'),
('127.751.d.21.F',4,'Mapping where and when crimes happen helps officers to:','["delay response", "focus prevention efforts", "avoid patrol", "guess"]'::jsonb,1,'Patterns guide action.','original'),
('127.751.d.22.A',3,'A first responder checks the rules for when to enter a crime scene, how long to remain, and when to release it. This identifies:','["traffic rules", "rumors", "legal requirements for first responders at a crime scene", "optional ideas"]'::jsonb,2,'Law controls scene access.','original'),
('127.751.d.22.A',4,'A first officer at a crime scene may enter without a warrant to:','["search everything", "clean up", "take souvenirs", "aid victims and make the scene safe"]'::jsonb,3,'Emergency aid is allowed.','original'),
('127.751.d.22.B',3,'Before stepping into a scene, a cadet puts on gloves, notes the entry path and tapes off the area. This demonstrates:','["procedures before entering and securing a crime scene", "contaminating", "skipping notes", "shortcuts"]'::jsonb,0,'Pre-entry steps protect evidence.','original'),
('127.751.d.22.B',4,'A crime scene boundary is set to:','["show off", "keep unauthorized people out and protect evidence", "help visitors", "delay the case"]'::jsonb,1,'Boundaries preserve evidence.','original'),
('127.751.d.22.C',3,'Students use a grid and spiral pattern to search a mock room for evidence. This demonstrates:','["random walking", "skipping areas", "a proper crime scene search", "tossing items"]'::jsonb,2,'Systematic searches avoid misses.','original'),
('127.751.d.22.C',4,'Which is a common pattern used to search a crime scene?','["a skip search", "a zigzag dance", "a random walk", "a grid or spiral search"]'::jsonb,3,'Patterns ensure coverage.','original'),
('127.751.d.22.D',3,'A technician places numbered tents beside each item before it is collected. This demonstrates:','["marking and collecting evidence", "ignoring items", "destroying evidence", "moving items"]'::jsonb,0,'Marking identifies items.','original'),
('127.751.d.22.D',4,'Before an item is moved, it is normally:','["discarded", "photographed and marked in place", "moved first", "cleaned"]'::jsonb,1,'Document position first.','original'),
('127.751.d.22.E',3,'A student measures from two fixed points to a bullet casing and sketches the room. This demonstrates:','["guessing", "painting", "measuring and sketching evidence and landmarks", "tracing"]'::jsonb,2,'Measurements fix locations.','original'),
('127.751.d.22.E',4,'Using two fixed reference points to record where an item lies is called:','["tetrahedra", "trisection", "trilogy", "triangulation"]'::jsonb,3,'Triangulation fixes position.','original'),
('127.751.d.22.F',3,'A photographer takes overall, mid-range and close-up pictures of a scene and a footprint with a scale. This demonstrates:','["photographing the scene and evidence", "editing", "sketching", "painting"]'::jsonb,0,'Photos document scenes.','original'),
('127.751.d.22.F',4,'A scale placed next to evidence in a photograph shows:','["its value", "the item''s actual size", "its age", "its owner"]'::jsonb,1,'Scales give size reference.','original'),
('127.751.d.22.G',3,'Each person who handles a bloody shirt signs a log, and it is sealed in a paper bag. This demonstrates:','["burning it", "tossing it", "chain of custody and proper packaging", "hiding it"]'::jsonb,2,'Custody documents who held it.','original'),
('127.751.d.22.G',4,'Biological evidence like a bloody shirt is usually packaged in:','["plastic bags", "tin cans", "glass jars", "paper bags"]'::jsonb,3,'Paper lets moisture escape.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
